  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.refusal_phrase_in. The documented rule reads: 'The first refusal phrase the text contains, typographic quotes read as straight ones, or None.' The two reply texts are: (1) 'I cannot assist. I am unable to help.' and (2) 'I am unable to help. I cannot assist.' For reply (1), the first refusal phrase in the text is 'I cannot', so the rule requires the answer 'I cannot'. For reply (2), the first refusal phrase in the text is 'I am unable', so the rule requires the answer 'I am unable'. The check, however, iterates the fixed list of refusal phrases in list order and returns the first list phrase that is a substring, so for both replies it returns 'I cannot', because 'I cannot' appears earlier in the list than 'I am unable'. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. The fix is to scan the text for the earliest occurrence among all phrases, not iterate the list. I would be wrong if the check, when run on these two texts, returns 'I cannot' for the first and 'I am unable' for the second, because that would show it is not collapsing the two replies to the same answer.",
